WEBVTT

00:02.400 --> 00:04.996
Figure twelve. Add a capability.

00:07.800 --> 00:25.684
The harbor repo has a backlog in beads, a dependency-aware issue tracker. Keelson knows nothing about it. The browser has Chat and Workflows, and the agent has nineteen tools, none of them for beads. Here is everything it takes to change that.

00:26.284 --> 00:34.568
No ribs installed. Add the beads rib from its GitHub repo. Bun installs it into Keelson's home, pinned to a release.

00:37.184 --> 00:42.092
Restart, and the server discovers it, checks it, and registers it.

00:43.184 --> 00:45.940
Fifteen tools, and four views.

00:46.484 --> 01:06.560
Those views ride on snapshots. A rib publishes data under its own keys, and the browser renders it as live boards. The rib never draws a pixel, opens a port, or touches the database. It hands over data and declarations, and the harness does the rest.

01:08.512 --> 01:14.892
So there's a Beads tab now. It found the harbor tracker on its own, and says what to start next.

01:16.968 --> 01:21.636
And chat can use the tools right away. No wiring, no config.

01:22.313 --> 01:26.631
It called two beads tools, and named the same two ready items.

01:28.070 --> 01:39.666
One command and a restart. A tab, fifteen tools, and four live views, and Keelson itself didn't change. Remove the rib, and they're gone.

01:40.266 --> 01:42.670
A capability is a dependency.

01:45.370 --> 01:48.808
Keelson. Lay the keel, raise the ribs.
